be preaching here at battelle chapel  where I spend so many sundays during my  undergraduate days in the late 1960s and  today as you know is all saints day when  the dead are figured Lee raised in the  tradition of Lazarus and I want in  particular to honor a man whom I'd  consider a modern-day Saint my  university chaplain and pastor during  those years William Sloane coffin jr. he  was a giant physically intellectually  spiritually and it was impossible not to  notice him and be affected by him no  matter who you were coming to Yale as a  self-proclaimed atheist I took a life  changing course with him in my freshman  year called a seminar for friendly  disbelievers learned about deep  religious confrontation with racism and  war in my sophomore and junior years and  then by senior year was a deacon here in  battelle Chapel it was coffins  inspiration that led me after divinity  and law schools to develop a legal  ministry for low-income residents in a  Boston neighborhood and the refrain that  I have remembered throughout my career  was Bill coffins justice not charity and  it's a topic on which I want to speak  today in terms of prophetic envisioning  of a new earth especially in terms of  the scandal of homelessness in America  Bill coffin called us to task in these  words many of us are eager to respond to  injustice as long as we can do so  without having to confront the causes of  that's the great pitfall of charity  handouts to needy individuals are  genuine necessary responses to injustice  but they do not necessarily face the  reason for injustice I started  practicing law at a time when there was  virtually no homelessness in America in  Boston anywhere in the nation I was  actually practicing housing law spending  a lot of time dealing with slumlords  with very difficult conditions that  people were living in but it was a very  different environment with very little  homelessness and then a political  decision was made in the early 1980s  part of a tax cutting regime to reduce  the federal housing budget from 33  billion dollars annually to seven  billion dollars and this was accompanied  by decisions made nationwide to d  institutionalize mental patients in  state hospitals without fulfilling the  promise that was made to them to fund  community mental health centers for  housing and a lot of mentally ill people  were simply discharged to the streets as  direct result of these two types of  political decisions now largely  forgotten it seems and never directly  experienced by this generation of  college students many people began going  homeless in Boston and all over America  forced to sleep in subways and parks on  heating grates strangely this was  happening at the same time as new  governmental incentives were being  implemented for business along with  general tax reductions helping fuel and  economic boom which resulted in enormous  increases in wealth for the already well  to do but not for the poor who lost  ground in the 1980s churches and other  charitable institutions were asked to  step in and provide shelters  and food pantries and help the homeless  and hungry at dramatic new levels  coffins response was that and I quote  the churches have to feed the hungry  clothe the naked and shelter the  homeless but they also have to remember  that the answer to homelessness is homes  not shelters when the poor and down  truck the poor and downtrodden need is  not piecemeal charity but wholesale  justice he taught that the homeless need  political action and structural changes  in society not just a warm bed and a  warm meal in a church basement quoting  him just to the slave just as to the to  the slave freedom was not a gift but the  restoration of a right no one had any  business taking from them so a home is a  right the homeless are being robbed  ironically when the rich take from the  poor it's called an economic plan when  the poor take from the rich it's called  class warfare coffin recalled that mr.  conservative Senator Robert Taft in the  1940s considered affordable housing a  moral imperative with the stated goal of  the 1948 Taft housing legislation being  a decent home for every American family  coffin was also ashamed that and i'm  quoting him I've listened to many a  Marxist accuse the churches of having a  vested interest in unjust structures  that produce victims to whom good  Christians can then pour out their  hearts in charity I've listened and I've  shuddered because so often in history  it's been so true bill coffin was a true  prophet a modern-day Isaiah challenging  the church as much as he did government  leaders and business people had i but  one wish  the churches of America he said I think  it would be that they come to see the  difference between charity and justice  charity is a matter of personal  attributes justice a matter of public  policy charity seeks to alleviate the  effects of injustice justice seeks to  eliminate the causes of it charity in no  way affects the status quo while justice  leads inevitably to political  confrontation he get down to both  trickle-down economics and the idea that  faith-based social services are enough  demanding in the words of the Prophet  Amos let justice roll down like waters  and righteousness like an ever-flowing  stream now obviously we're in much more  difficult economic times over the last  year than we've been for certainly any  time in my lifetime and homelessness has  increased and it's more entrenched than  ever was particularly struck by this in  a trip that I took to Chicago just last  weekend although we have plenty of  homeless people in Palo Alto California  as well not long ago we opened an  opportunity center near the Stanford  campus to provide resources and housing  to this population we reading the book  of Acts that the early Christian  communities in in the early Christian  communities none were needy because they  would sell their possessions and goods  and distribute the proceeds to all as  any had need but the societal answer now  well beyond voluntary giving and mutual  support in Christian communities is  legislation that covers each and every  citizen equally as a matter of social  justice of course there should be  universal health care in America as  there is in virtually ever every other  industrialized country in the world  there should be protection for  homeowners who received subprime  mortgage mortgage loans in good faith  and there should be continued extension  of unemployment benefits for those who  have been laid off during this time but  importantly in terms of what I'm saying  today we created the homelessness crisis  in America by a series of political  decisions in the 1980s and we can end it  today by political decisions that we can  make from a religious perspective we  have to understand that we're all in  this together what the Apostle Paul says  of the body of Christ that is the church  we must now say of the whole world the  body does not consist of one member but  many the I cannot say to the hand I have  no need of you nor again to the head to  the feet I have no need of you if one  member suffers all suffer together with  it if one member is honored all rejoice  together in it times like this we must  remember how deeply interlocked and  interdependent we are we need to develop  the ability to talk to one another  across all divisions of age and  occupation and ethnicity and class about  our common plight and our vision of  solutions for the common good and that  should happen in grocery stores and on  the streets and in our neighborhoods on  the playing fields of subway in school  wherever people gather outside of our  normal insularity how ironic then that  words like class warfare we utilize  during the 2008 presidential campaign  after the economic crisis began with  predictably divisive effect and how many  times that we heard our current  president condemned as a socialist  Christianity in this age must remind us  of the truth of Jesus's words that we  cannot serve both God and wealth money  or the fantasy of economic security may  always stand as the greatest temptation  to violate the fur  of the Ten Commandments i am the lord  your god you shall have no other gods  before me you shall not make for  yourself an idol it's the golden calf  which we risk bowing down to and  worshipping every day ultimately what  matters most from a Christian  perspective is love and community  including a deep and abiding commitment  to social justice charity may provide  band-aids to the effect of poverty but  only justice seeks to eliminate its  causes charity as it's often been said  may lead people to pull a drowning baby  out of a river and pulling a second and  a third baby floating down behind it but  justice requires that we go upstream and  see why those babies are ending up in  the river in the first place and  eliminating the problem at its source  charity yes always bill coffin said from  this pulpit but never as a substitute  for justice what we keep forgetting in  this country is that people have basic  rights the right to food the right to  decent housing the right to medical care  the right to education close quote maybe  we recognize the love which is our  birthright and our calling may we take  joy in human caring and recognizing the  kinship that we all share and then we  move beyond mere charity to a commitment  to justice in the words the apostle  Michael what does the Lord require of  you but to do justice and to love  kindness and to walk humbly with our God  amen
 

